    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. Could you go into a bit more detail on the specific steps within the RAAS cascade? Like, specifically how the reduction in Angiotensin II levels affects the adrenal cortex and what that does to sodium and water retention? I want to make sure I have the whole chain of events down for the exam.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors effectively stop the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II. This results in decreased levels of angiotensin II, a key component in the RAAS cascade. With less angiotensin II, the adrenal cortex produces less aldosterone, leading to decreased sodium reabsorption and increased sodium excretion in the urine. As a result, water follows the sodium in the urine, decreasing water reabsorption and promoting diuresis. Less aldosterone also reduces sodium and water retention, ultimately leading to decreased blood volume and subsequently lower blood pressure.",
      "source": "target",
